Z||000019||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||07-17-2001||02:40 AM||||Hey, manufacturers put "all show, no go" stickers on cars all the time.<P>How about the 86 Hyundai excel GT, 88 Chevy Nova SS, 84 Ford EXP, and tons of others?<P>What gets me is when I see a Honda civic VTEC si turbo or any other bizzare combo.  But what about the "super chickens" Pontiac put on late 70s Trans Ams?  Those cars were covered in stickers.  Ever seen a 1976 Mustang Cobra II?  Wait, weren't there lots of 84 Fieros with stickers all over them?<BR>I like my cars plain.  <P>To me, the "ricer" thing is about cutting coil springs and three-level wings.  It manifests as three-blade wipers that come in neon green and the "Calvin pissing on something" stickers.  There is a late model Honda civic at work with every piece of the interior and exterior painted white.  It looks odd.<P>And, then there are the Integras with 8" exhaust tips, neon yellow or green paint, and those little Japanese letters.  Some of these cars are pretty, but so gaudy and cartoonish.  I don't like them, but some of them have impressed me with how well they were done.<P>Ricers, "Bitchin' Camaros", Mini-trucks, Datsun 510s, hot-rodders, etc.<P>Since the 1900s people have loved their cars.  It's the ones that get carried away with it that we remember.  <P>Just wait until the new "rugged sporty hatchback" craze hits.  The Pontiac Vibe is due out soon . . .  What will we call them?  <p>[This message has been edited by LuckyTheyWereEverMade (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000020||firstfiero||07-17-2001||09:40 AM||||    You can do what you want simple fiero but I call them ricers. It has nothing to do with the brand of car they drive!! It's what you do to it.<BR>    I forgot I even posted on this thread  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Somebody is always getting their little panties in an up roar on this forum lately.<BR><P>------------------<BR><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/firstfiero.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||1FST2M6||07-17-2001||03:18 PM||||Generally, being 'ricey' centers around having a lot of Touring style appearance items without any actual performance modifications, but that's not the whole story. Just adding gargantuan exhaust tips, tall spoilers, multitudinous stickers, ground effects, driving lights, hood pins, huge wheels (and calling them rims), etc. doesn't automatically mean you are 'rice'. Style, after all, is a personal thing. <BR>Rice is more of an attitude problem. Ricers confuse appearance with reality, and convince themselves that a car that looks fast is fast. Talking the talk without enough performance work to walk the walk is what makes someone a true Riceboy or Ricegirl. <P>The term 'rice' itself derives from the predominantly Japanese cars favored by the early trendsetters of this style, and the graphics of untranslatable Asian characters that they often carry. It is not a reference to Asian people in general.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||Ferrari_cdn||07-17-2001||03:31 PM||||Mach10:  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>you just made a 4-lane change without signals in front of me<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>*sigh* TOO common.  I see it all the time at late night weekend driving on Portage.<P>BTW, if you know why, whats going on on Sunday night, everyone's sitting on parking lots around Portage between Polo Park and Downtown, watching the streets?||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||07-17-2001||03:40 PM||||Cruise night, m'man! In polo park, it's either the Muscle or the Rice crowd, depending on who gets there first. Burger Factory is another hangout.<BR>We should get a whole bunch (6) of Pegger Fieros together on sundays. Mine's getting paint today (car less for 2 days... *sniff*)<P>I just HATE the rice exhaust. Having that exhaust system is an attitude problem in itself. It shows a lack of consideration for people that don't want to hear your annoying buzzy car. Every system that I've seen resonates BADLY and makes the sounds louder AND more obnoxious.||||reg||7
